<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jerónimo de Aguilar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1489–<a href="/years/1531">1531</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jerónimo de Aguilar was a Spanish Franciscan who was shipwrecked on the coast of Yucatán in 1511 and lived among the Maya for eight years. Rescued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> at Cozumel in 1519, he served as interpreter, and working with La Malinche, who translated between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> and Maya, he made communication with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a> possible during the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jeronimo-de-aguilar.jpg" alt="Jerónimo de Aguilar" width="320" height="459" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fondo Antiguo de la Biblioteca de la Universidad de Sevilla from Sevilla, España · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Historia_de_la_conquista_de_M%C3%A9jico,_1851_%22Ger%C3%B3nimo_de_Aguilar_es_presentado_a_Cort%C3%A9s_despu%C3%A9s_de_ocho_a%C3%B1os_de_esclavitud_entre_los_indios%22._(3965822244).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
